 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Precomputation of the 3D-filter is expensive but done once. Rendering time is linear in the number of voxels, as it involves multiplying precomputed form factors by voxel energies and accumulating [S1][S2, book].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the 3D-filter (extended form factors) for each voxel. The size depends on the number of contributing voxels per filter and the scattering orders considered [S1][S2, book].
-*   **Strengths:** Efficiently handles multiple scattering in dense media like clouds and snow. Reduces computational cost by limiting scattering orders and using stochastic sampling for form factor computation [S1][S2, book].
-*   **Limitations:** Limited to the first three scattering orders. Accuracy depends on the forward-scattering assumption. Less flexible for arbitrary phase functions or non-cloud-like media [S1][S2, book].
-*   **When to Use:** Rendering clouds and snow where multiple scattering is dominant and forward scattering is a valid approximation [S1][S2, book].
-*   **When Not to Use:** Media with strong backscattering or where higher-order scattering effects are critical [S1][S2, book].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The method is faster than explicit multiple scattering path tracing because it precomputes the 3D-filter and uses stochastic selection to limit the number of voxel interactions considered for second and third-order scattering [S2, book].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the voxel grid and the 3D-filter data structure [S2, book].
+*   **Strengths:** Efficiently handles multiple scattering in clouds and snow (high albedo media) by separating single and multiple scattering components and leveraging forward scattering properties [S1][S2, book].
+*   **Limitations:** Approximate; limited to second and third-order scattering; assumes forward scattering; less accurate for highly heterogeneous or non-forward scattering media [S1][S2, book].
+*   **When to Use:** Rendering clouds and snow where multiple scattering is visually significant but full path tracing is too expensive [S1][S2, book].
+*   **When Not to Use:** Media with strong backscattering or where higher-order multiple scattering is critical [S2, book].
 
 ## Background
-The 3D-Filter method was proposed by Nishita et al. to render clouds with multiple scattering and sky light [S1][S2, book]. It was later applied to render snow, which has a high albedo and appears white due to multiple scattering [S1]. The shape and density distribution of snow in these applications were modeled using metaballs [S1]. The method is part of a broader class of techniques that use implicit representations of directional distribution, such as scattering patterns or diffusion equations [S1].
+The 3D-Filter method was proposed by Nishita et al. for displaying clouds taking into account multiple scattering and sky light [S2, book]. It was also applied to render snow, which has a high albedo and looks white due to multiple scattering, with shape and density modeled using metaballs [S1]. The method is part of a broader family of techniques that use implicit representations of directional distribution, such as scattering patterns and diffusion equations [S1][S2, book]. Harris and Lastra also considered second-order scattering for a reduced number of incoming directions in related work [S1].
 
